course: dictionary
lesson: 15762
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words can do more than one job, and their meaning changes with the words around them. Trouser is a good example. You usually see it as «trousers», the clothes you wear on your legs, but the single word «trouser» can come before another noun to tell you what kind of thing it is: «trouser pocket».
explain: «Mia put her key in her trouser pocket.» Here trouser is not a thing you wear. It tells you which pocket it is: the one that belongs to a pair of trousers. Ask yourself what the word after it is, and what it belongs to.
explain: In British English, trouser can also be a verb, and it is informal. «The clerk trousered the cash and left.» The clues are cash and left: the clerk took money and kept it, often in a sly or unfair way. Check your meaning by swapping it in: pocketed the cash, does that still make sense? It does.
fact: «Trouser» before a noun means having to do with trousers.
fact: «Trouser leg», «trouser pocket» and «trouser press» are phrases where trouser comes before another noun.
fact: «Trousers» is the everyday word for the clothing that covers each leg. In America it is called «pants».
fact: As a verb, «trousered» means took money and kept it, often in a sly or unfair way.
fact: Informal means relaxed, everyday language, not the careful language of formal writing.
exercise: «The mud stained his trouser leg.» In the phrase trouser leg, what does trouser mean? | *Having to do with trousers | To take money and keep it | A type of shoe | A kind of hat
exercise: «The thief trousered the money from the till.» What does trousered mean here? | Washed the money carefully | *Took the money and kept it, often unfairly | Counted the money twice | Gave the money back to the bank
exercise: «Sam felt for his coin in his trouser pocket.» Which word comes before the noun pocket? | Sam | felt | coin | *trouser
test: «Dad trousered the prize money and said nothing.» What does trousered suggest? | He lost the money in the park | *He took the money and kept it | He shared the money with everyone | He counted the money out loud
test: «The trouser press made his suit smooth.» From its name, what is a trouser press? | *A press for trousers | A pair of shoes | A hot drink | A kind of bicycle
exam: In which sentence does trouser mean to take money and keep it? | «The trouser pocket was torn.» | *«He trousered the coins from the till.» | «The trouser leg was muddy.»
